  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find the coefficient of $(x-3)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ about $x=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-3)^{3}$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x-3)^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(3).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2034}(3),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2034}(3)=-\\frac{6}{{3}^{4}}=-\\frac{6}{81}=-\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(3)=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{27})=-\\frac{2}{6\\cdot 27}=-\\frac{1}{81}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $(x-3)^{3}$ is $-\\frac{1}{81}.$",
